export function structures(add,cpp) {
const nodes="struct Node {\n    int value;\n    std::unique_ptr<Node> next;\n};\nvoid prepend(std::unique_ptr<Node> &head, int value) {\n    auto node = std::make_unique<Node>(Node{value, nullptr});\n    node->next = std::move(head);\n    head = std::move(node);\n}";
add('lists-queues','owned-list','Own each linked node exactly once',
'A singly linked list can represent structural ownership with unique_ptr links. The head owns the first node and each node owns the next. Traversal uses borrowed pointers that never delete nodes.\n\nThis small acyclic chain delegates cleanup to the ownership structure. Very long recursive destruction chains may need an iterative teardown strategy.',
cpp('iostream memory utility',"    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;\n    prepend(head, 2);\n    prepend(head, 1);\n    for (const Node *node = head.get(); node; node = node->next.get())\n        std::cout << node->value;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";",nodes),'12',
'Prepending transfers the old head into the new node before publishing the new head. Traversal borrows each node while the list stays alive.',
'Two owning pointers to the same node would violate unique ownership and could double-delete it.',
['Who owns the second node?','The first node’s next member','The traversal pointer','Every pointer that reaches it'],'The chain has one owning link per node.',
'Add a third node and draw owners separately from observer pointers. Explain why an owning back-link would create a different lifetime problem.');
add('lists-queues','remove-owned-node','Detach an owner before destroying a node',
'Removing a head node requires transferring ownership of its successor before the old head is destroyed. Use a local owner to keep the removed node alive while its next link is detached.\n\nAfter removal, any observer of the removed node is invalid. An observer of a surviving node remains useful only while its new owner chain stays alive.',
cpp('iostream memory utility',"    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;\n    prepend(head, 2);\n    prepend(head, 1);\n    auto removed = std::move(head);\n    head = std::move(removed->next);\n    removed.reset();\n    std::cout << head->value << \"\\n\";",nodes),'2',
'The old head is held in removed. Its successor moves into head before the old node is released.',
'Do not read a member through a node after replacing and destroying the owner of that node.',
['Why keep the removed node in a local owner?','To detach its successor while the node is still alive','To create shared ownership','To copy the entire list'],'The local establishes the required lifetime during the ownership transfer.',
'Handle an empty list and a single-node list. Return an optional removed value without returning a pointer to the destroyed node.');
add('lists-queues','lifo-stack','A stack exposes last-in, first-out behavior',
'A stack restricts operations to the most recently added item. This matches nested work such as undo history or an explicit replacement for recursion. The backing container’s broader operations should not leak into a narrow stack contract.\n\nCheck emptiness before observing or removing the top item; standard adaptors do not return an optional for an empty pop.',
cpp('iostream stack',"    std::stack<int> work;\n    work.push(1);\n    work.push(2);\n    work.push(3);\n    while (!work.empty()) {\n        std::cout << work.top();\n        work.pop();\n    }\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";"),'321',
'The most recently pushed value is removed first, producing the reverse insertion order.',
'Calling top on an empty stack violates its precondition.',
['Which item is removed first?','The last item pushed','The first item pushed','The smallest item'],'LIFO refers to insertion history rather than numeric order.',
'Implement the same interface with a vector and compare the empty-state contract. Use the stack to traverse a small tree iteratively.');
const ring="struct Queue {\n    std::array<int, 3> data{};\n    std::size_t head = 0, count = 0;\n    bool push(int value) {\n        if (count == data.size())\n            return false;\n        data[(head + count) % data.size()] = value;\n        ++count;\n        return true;\n    }\n    std::optional<int> pop() {\n        if (!count)\n            return std::nullopt;\n        int value = data[head];\n        head = (head + 1) % data.size();\n        --count;\n        return value;\n    }\n};";
add('lists-queues','ring-buffer','Separate a ring’s logical order from its array positions',
'A bounded ring queue reuses an array by wrapping physical positions. The logical head and live count determine the next insertion slot. Count distinguishes empty from full even when modular positions coincide.\n\nReject a full insertion before changing any queue state. This makes the failure behavior easy to test.',
cpp('array iostream optional',"    Queue queue;\n    queue.push(1);\n    queue.push(2);\n    queue.push(3);\n    std::cout << \"full=\" << !queue.push(4) << \" first=\" << *queue.pop() << \"\\n\";\n    queue.push(4);\n    while (auto value = queue.pop())\n        std::cout << *value;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";",ring),'full=1 first=1\n234',
'After removing one, insertion wraps to the freed slot. The remaining logical order is two, three, four.',
'Comparing head and tail positions without another state rule can confuse full with empty.',
['What disambiguates full and empty here?','The live count','The values stored in unused slots','The array address'],'Count is zero when empty and capacity when full.',
'Test several complete wrap cycles and a rejected insertion. Compare every popped value with a deque reference model.');
add('lists-queues','fifo-deque','A queue preserves arrival order',
'A FIFO queue removes the oldest pending item. deque supports efficient insertion at one end and removal at the other without requiring every remaining element to shift.\n\nThe container alone does not provide a concurrent shutdown protocol. Later queue lessons add synchronization, close state, and draining rules.',
cpp('deque iostream',"    std::deque<int> pending{10, 20};\n    pending.push_back(30);\n    while (!pending.empty()) {\n        std::cout << pending.front() << \" \";\n        pending.pop_front();\n    }\n    std::cout << \"empty=\" << pending.empty() << \"\\n\";"),'10 20 30 empty=1',
'The output preserves insertion order and the final queue is empty.',
'A thread-safe allocator does not make unsynchronized deque operations thread-safe.',
['What additional contract does a concurrent queue need?','Synchronization and shutdown behavior','A different integer type only','A promise that allocation never occurs'],'Container ownership and inter-thread coordination are separate responsibilities.',
'Add a bounded capacity rule to a queue wrapper. Specify whether full insertion rejects, blocks, or drops an item.');

const hash="template <std::size_t N> struct Hash {\n    enum class State { empty, full, deleted };\n    struct Slot {\n        State state = State::empty;\n        unsigned key = 0;\n        int value = 0;\n    };\n    std::array<Slot, N> slots{};\n    bool put(unsigned key, int value) {\n        std::optional<std::size_t> free;\n        for (std::size_t step = 0; step < N; ++step) {\n            auto at = (key % N + step) % N;\n            auto &slot = slots[at];\n            if (slot.state == State::full && slot.key == key)\n                return false;\n            if (slot.state != State::full && !free)\n                free = at;\n            if (slot.state == State::empty)\n                break;\n        }\n        if (!free)\n            return false;\n        slots[*free] = {State::full, key, value};\n        return true;\n    }\n    std::optional<int> get(unsigned key) const {\n        for (std::size_t step = 0; step < N; ++step) {\n            const auto &slot = slots[(key % N + step) % N];\n            if (slot.state == State::empty)\n                return std::nullopt;\n            if (slot.state == State::full && slot.key == key)\n                return slot.value;\n        }\n        return std::nullopt;\n    }\n    bool erase(unsigned key) {\n        for (std::size_t step = 0; step < N; ++step) {\n            auto &slot = slots[(key % N + step) % N];\n            if (slot.state == State::empty)\n                return false;\n            if (slot.state == State::full && slot.key == key) {\n                slot.state = State::deleted;\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n};";
add('hashing','hash-buckets','A hash chooses a bucket, not a unique identity',
'Different keys can map to the same bucket. A correct hash table must resolve collisions and still compare the full key. This teaching table uses key modulo capacity so collision fixtures are explicit and portable.\n\nThe remainder function is not presented as a production-quality general-purpose hash. Its role is to expose the collision protocol.',
cpp('array iostream optional',"    Hash<7> table;\n    table.put(1, 10);\n    table.put(8, 80);\n    std::cout << \"bucket=\" << 1 % 7 << \",\" << 8 % 7 << \" values=\" << *table.get(1)\n              << \",\" << *table.get(8) << \"\\n\";",hash),'bucket=1,1 values=10,80',
'Keys one and eight start in the same bucket but retain different payloads through probing and key comparison.',
'Treating equal hash values as equal keys loses distinct records.',
['Do equal bucket numbers imply equal keys?','No','Yes','Only at low load'],'Collisions are expected and require full-key comparison.',
'Insert several keys separated by the capacity and inspect the probe sequence. State how absence is detected.');
add('hashing','linear-probing','Bound a probe sequence by the table capacity',
'Linear probing checks successive slots with wraparound. A bounded table must stop after at most capacity inspections even when every slot has been occupied.\n\nInsertion tracks a reusable slot but continues far enough to detect an existing duplicate key. A full-table rejection must leave the current mappings unchanged.',
cpp('array iostream optional',"    Hash<3> table;\n    table.put(0, 10);\n    table.put(3, 30);\n    table.put(6, 60);\n    std::cout << \"full=\" << !table.put(9, 90) << \" absent=\" << !table.get(9) << \"\\n\";",hash),'full=1 absent=1',
'All three colliding slots are occupied. Both insertion and missing lookup terminate after a bounded probe sequence.',
'A probe loop that waits only for an empty slot can run forever when the table is full.',
['What bounds unsuccessful probing?','At most N inspected slots','The numeric value of the key','A required empty sentinel outside the array'],'The loop explicitly counts at most capacity probes.',
'Fill the table in several insertion orders and verify unsuccessful operations always terminate and preserve existing values.');
add('hashing','tombstones','Deletion must preserve later probe paths',
'A deleted slot cannot always be changed to never-used empty state. Other keys may have crossed it during insertion, so stopping there would hide live entries. A tombstone records reusable storage while allowing lookup to continue.\n\nTombstones affect performance and eventual rebuild policy, but they are first a correctness issue.',
cpp('array iostream optional',"    Hash<7> table;\n    table.put(1, 10);\n    table.put(8, 80);\n    table.erase(1);\n    std::cout << \"deleted=\" << !table.get(1) << \" survivor=\" << *table.get(8) << \"\\n\";",hash),'deleted=1 survivor=80',
'Lookup crosses the deleted first slot and still finds key eight farther along the probe path.',
'Marking the slot as never-used empty would incorrectly terminate the survivor lookup.',
['Why does lookup cross a tombstone?','A live collision may lie beyond it','The tombstone still owns a row','Deleted keys compare equal to every key'],'Deletion must retain the history needed by probing.',
'Reuse the tombstone with a new colliding key. Test duplicate rejection even when an earlier tombstone is available.');
add('hashing','rehashing','Rebuild an index without changing its logical mappings',
'Changing capacity changes bucket selection, so entries must be reinserted into a new table rather than copied to the same slot numbers. Build a candidate index and publish it only after all required mappings have been transferred.\n\nThis fixture uses two compile-time capacities to make the rebuild explicit. A dynamic implementation can own a resizable slot vector.',
cpp('array iostream optional',"    Hash<3> old;\n    old.put(1, 10);\n    old.put(4, 40);\n    Hash<7> candidate;\n    for (const auto &slot : old.slots)\n        if (slot.state == Hash<3>::State::full)\n            candidate.put(slot.key, slot.value);\n    std::cout << *candidate.get(1) << \" \" << *candidate.get(4) << \"\\n\";",hash),'10 40',
'The logical key/value pairs survive despite different bucket and probe positions in the new capacity.',
'Copying raw slots into a differently sized table breaks the new table’s lookup assumptions.',
['What must remain invariant across rehashing?','The key/value mappings','Every physical slot number','The old modulo divisor'],'Rehashing changes representation while preserving logical contents.',
'Inject a candidate-capacity failure and keep the old table intact. Check every transferred key against an independent map.');
add('hashing','hash-reference','Check a hash implementation against an independent model',
'A reference ordered map can validate the observable behavior of a small custom hash table. Use mixed collisions, deletion, missing lookups, and reinsertion rather than only successful unique inserts.\n\nThe oracle should compare mappings, not duplicate the probe implementation. That permits different internal layouts while enforcing the same external contract.',
cpp('array iostream map optional',"    Hash<7> table;\n    std::map<unsigned, int> model;\n    for (unsigned key : {1u, 8u, 2u}) {\n        table.put(key, int(key * 10));\n        model.emplace(key, int(key * 10));\n    }\n    table.erase(8);\n    model.erase(8);\n    bool correct = true;\n    for (unsigned key = 0; key < 10; ++key) {\n        auto actual = table.get(key);\n        auto found = model.find(key);\n        correct = correct && (bool(actual) == (found != model.end())) &&\n                  (!actual || *actual == found->second);\n    }\n    std::cout << \"model=\" << correct << \"\\n\";\n    return correct ? 0 : 1;",hash),'model=1',
'Every queried key agrees on presence and, when present, payload. The model uses tree lookup rather than probing.',
'A reference that repeats the same collision logic can reproduce the same bug.',
['Why is std::map a useful oracle here?','It has different representation and lookup logic','It uses the same probe slots','It guarantees this hash is fast'],'Independent behavior is more valuable than mirrored internals.',
'Enumerate short operation sequences and compare after every step, including full-table and duplicate insertion failures.');

const avl="struct Node {\n    int key, height = 1;\n    std::unique_ptr<Node> left, right;\n    explicit Node(int value) : key(value) {\n    }\n};\nusing Tree = std::unique_ptr<Node>;\nint height(const Tree &node) {\n    return node ? node->height : 0;\n}\nvoid update(Node &node) {\n    node.height = 1 + std::max(height(node.left), height(node.right));\n}\nTree left_rotate(Tree root) {\n    auto raised = std::move(root->right);\n    root->right = std::move(raised->left);\n    update(*root);\n    raised->left = std::move(root);\n    update(*raised);\n    return raised;\n}\nTree right_rotate(Tree root) {\n    auto raised = std::move(root->left);\n    root->left = std::move(raised->right);\n    update(*root);\n    raised->right = std::move(root);\n    update(*raised);\n    return raised;\n}\nvoid balance(Tree &root) {\n    if (!root)\n        return;\n    update(*root);\n    if (height(root->left) - height(root->right) > 1) {\n        if (height(root->left->right) > height(root->left->left))\n            root->left = left_rotate(std::move(root->left));\n        root = right_rotate(std::move(root));\n    } else if (height(root->right) - height(root->left) > 1) {\n        if (height(root->right->left) > height(root->right->right))\n            root->right = right_rotate(std::move(root->right));\n        root = left_rotate(std::move(root));\n    }\n}\nvoid insert(Tree &root, int key) {\n    if (!root) {\n        root = std::make_unique<Node>(key);\n        return;\n    }\n    if (key < root->key)\n        insert(root->left, key);\n    else if (key > root->key)\n        insert(root->right, key);\n    balance(root);\n}\nbool contains(const Tree &root, int key) {\n    const Node *node = root.get();\n    while (node) {\n        if (key == node->key)\n            return true;\n        node = key < node->key ? node->left.get() : node->right.get();\n    }\n    return false;\n}\nvoid erase(Tree &root, int key) {\n    if (!root)\n        return;\n    if (key < root->key)\n        erase(root->left, key);\n    else if (key > root->key)\n        erase(root->right, key);\n    else if (!root->left) {\n        auto next = std::move(root->right);\n        root = std::move(next);\n    } else if (!root->right) {\n        auto next = std::move(root->left);\n        root = std::move(next);\n    } else {\n        const Node *next = root->right.get();\n        while (next->left)\n            next = next->left.get();\n        root->key = next->key;\n        erase(root->right, next->key);\n    }\n    balance(root);\n}\nvoid ordered(const Tree &root, std::vector<int> &result) {\n    if (!root)\n        return;\n    ordered(root->left, result);\n    result.push_back(root->key);\n    ordered(root->right, result);\n}\nbool heights_valid(const Tree &root) {\n    return !root ||\n           (root->height == 1 + std::max(height(root->left), height(root->right)) &&\n            std::abs(height(root->left) - height(root->right)) <= 1 &&\n            heights_valid(root->left) && heights_valid(root->right));\n}";
const ah='algorithm cmath iostream memory utility vector';
add('balanced-trees','tree-ordering','Preserve the search-tree ordering invariant',
'A binary search tree partitions keys around each node: smaller keys go left and larger keys go right. Ownership follows child links while searches borrow nodes. In-order traversal should therefore produce unique sorted keys.\n\nThe shared implementation is an AVL tree; the following lessons examine how rotations preserve this ordering while controlling height.',
cpp(ah,"    Tree root;\n    for (int key : {4, 2, 6, 1, 3})\n        insert(root, key);\n    std::vector<int> values;\n    ordered(root, values);\n    for (int key : values)\n        std::cout << key;\n    std::cout << \" found=\" << contains(root, 3) << \"\\n\";",avl),'12346 found=1',
'Traversal visits left subtree, node, then right subtree. Lookup follows the same ordering rather than scanning the complete tree.',
'A balanced shape alone does not prove key ordering or reachability.',
['Which traversal exposes sorted key order?','In-order traversal','Root-only traversal','Following only right children'],'The ordering invariant places each node between its left and right descendants.',
'Insert duplicates and absent-search keys. Compare traversal with an independently maintained sorted set.');
add('balanced-trees','avl-height','Keep stored heights consistent with structure',
'AVL balancing requires each node’s child heights to differ by at most one. Stored heights must also equal one plus the maximum child height. A stale height can make a later balancing decision wrong even when current output appears sorted.\n\nThe empty subtree has height zero in this implementation, so a leaf has height one.',
cpp(ah,"    Tree root;\n    for (int key : {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7})\n        insert(root, key);\n    std::cout << \"root=\" << root->key << \" height=\" << height(root)\n              << \" valid=\" << heights_valid(root) << \"\\n\";",avl),'root=4 height=3 valid=1',
'Increasing insertions trigger rotations; the final seven-key tree has root four and height three.',
'Checking only sorted traversal can miss incorrect stored heights that break future updates.',
['What height does an empty subtree have here?','Zero','One','The parent height'],'The height convention makes leaf height one.',
'Validate heights after every insertion, not only at the end. Try reversed and zigzag insertion orders.');
add('balanced-trees','avl-single-rotation','A rotation transfers ownership while preserving order',
'A single rotation raises a child and moves the child’s inner subtree to the old root. The sorted in-order sequence stays unchanged while the height imbalance is reduced.\n\nunique_ptr moves make each structural ownership transfer explicit. Rotations do not allocate new nodes or copy owned subtrees.',
cpp(ah,"    Tree ascending, descending;\n    for (int key : {10, 20, 30})\n        insert(ascending, key);\n    for (int key : {30, 20, 10})\n        insert(descending, key);\n    std::cout << ascending->key << \" \" << descending->key << \"\\n\";",avl),'20 20',
'Opposite skew directions require opposite rotations. Both produce the middle key as root.',
'Forgetting to reconnect the inner subtree can leak reachability or lose keys even if the new root looks correct.',
['Do rotations need new node allocations here?','No','Yes, one per key','Only for a right rotation'],'They transfer existing owning links.',
'Add a nonempty inner subtree and verify every key remains reachable after rotation. Compare the complete in-order sequences.');
add('balanced-trees','avl-double-rotation','Resolve a zigzag imbalance in two steps',
'A left-right or right-left insertion creates an inner-heavy child. First rotate that child into an outer-heavy shape, then rotate the parent. Both updates must recompute affected heights in dependency order.\n\nTreat the two rotations as structural operations with the same ordering invariant rather than as a special-case key swap.',
cpp(ah,"    Tree root;\n    for (int key : {30, 10, 20})\n        insert(root, key);\n    std::cout << root->key << \" left=\" << root->left->key\n              << \" right=\" << root->right->key << \" valid=\" << heights_valid(root)\n              << \"\\n\";",avl),'20 left=10 right=30 valid=1',
'The middle key becomes root after a child rotation and a parent rotation, with both side subtrees retained.',
'Swapping only key values does not generally repair subtree ownership or balancing.',
['Why rotate the child first?','To convert the zigzag into a case a parent rotation can repair','To allocate a new root','To remove the smallest key'],'The first rotation changes the structural orientation of the heavy path.',
'Test the mirrored right-left case. Verify ordering and heights independently after both patterns.');
add('balanced-trees','avl-delete-oracle','Deletion can require rebalancing toward the root',
'Deleting a key can reduce a subtree’s height and create imbalances above the removed node. A two-child deletion replaces the key with an in-order successor and then removes that successor from its old location.\n\nValidate both the set of keys and the height/balance invariants. Either check alone can miss a different class of structural bug.',
cpp(ah,"    Tree root;\n    for (int key : {4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7})\n        insert(root, key);\n    erase(root, 4);\n    erase(root, 1);\n    std::vector<int> actual;\n    ordered(root, actual);\n    const std::vector<int> expected{2, 3, 5, 6, 7};\n    bool valid = actual == expected && heights_valid(root);\n    std::cout << \"delete verified=\" << valid << \"\\n\";\n    return valid ? 0 : 1;",avl),'delete verified=1',
'The root-key and leaf deletions retain exactly the expected surviving keys and valid AVL heights.',
'Deletion must not leave an observer pointing to a removed node or skip rebalancing an ancestor.',
['Which checks are both needed?','Key contents and structural invariants','Only the root key','Only the number of allocations'],'Correct ordering, membership, heights, and ownership are separate properties.',
'Enumerate insertion permutations for four keys and delete every key in turn. Compare every intermediate state with std::set.');

const heap="struct MinHeap {\n    std::vector<int> values;\n    void push(int value) {\n        values.push_back(value);\n        auto at = values.size() - 1;\n        while (at > 0) {\n            auto parent = (at - 1) / 2;\n            if (values[parent] <= values[at])\n                break;\n            std::swap(values[parent], values[at]);\n            at = parent;\n        }\n    }\n    std::optional<int> pop() {\n        if (values.empty())\n            return std::nullopt;\n        int result = values.front();\n        values.front() = values.back();\n        values.pop_back();\n        std::size_t at = 0;\n        while (at < values.size()) {\n            auto child = at * 2 + 1;\n            if (child >= values.size())\n                break;\n            if (child + 1 < values.size() && values[child + 1] < values[child])\n                ++child;\n            if (values[at] <= values[child])\n                break;\n            std::swap(values[at], values[child]);\n            at = child;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n    bool valid() const {\n        for (std::size_t i = 1; i < values.size(); ++i)\n            if (values[(i - 1) / 2] > values[i])\n                return false;\n        return true;\n    }\n};";
add('heaps','heap-order','A heap maintains a partial ordering',
'In a binary min-heap, every parent is no greater than its children. This guarantees a minimum at the root without fully sorting the array. Child positions follow arithmetic indices in the owned vector.\n\nHeap validation should compare every parent-child edge, not require the entire array to be sorted.',
cpp('iostream optional utility vector',"    MinHeap heap;\n    for (int value : {5, 2, 8, 1})\n        heap.push(value);\n    std::cout << \"minimum=\" << heap.values.front() << \" valid=\" << heap.valid() << \"\\n\";",heap),'minimum=1 valid=1',
'Insertion sifts a new value toward its parent until the heap condition is restored.',
'A valid heap is not necessarily globally sorted in array order.',
['What does the heap invariant guarantee?','A minimum at the root','Every adjacent array pair is ordered','All leaves have equal values'],'The parent-child relation establishes the root minimum.',
'Print the internal array and compare heap validity with global sortedness. Explain why they are different properties.');
add('heaps','sift-up','Restore order after appending a leaf',
'A newly appended heap element can violate order only along its ancestor path. Sift-up swaps it with a larger parent until the local relation holds or it reaches the root.\n\nThe vector owns all elements throughout the operation. An index is reacquired after push_back, avoiding observers into an allocation that may have moved.',
cpp('iostream optional utility vector',"    MinHeap heap;\n    for (int value : {7, 6, 5, 4}) {\n        heap.push(value);\n        std::cout << heap.values.front();\n    }\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";",heap),'7654',
'Each newly inserted smaller value travels to the root through its parent path.',
'Computing (at-1)/2 when at is unsigned zero underflows before division.',
['Why test at>0 before computing the parent?','To avoid unsigned underflow at the root','To prevent duplicate keys','To force every insertion to allocate'],'The root has no parent.',
'Count swaps for increasing and decreasing insertions. Relate the maximum path length to the heap height.');
add('heaps','sift-down','Restore order after removing the minimum',
'Removing the root leaves a hole filled by the final element. Only a downward path can now violate min-heap order. Choose the smaller child before deciding whether to swap.\n\nThe empty case returns no result. The single-element case removes the final value without reading a nonexistent child.',
cpp('iostream optional utility vector',"    MinHeap heap;\n    for (int value : {8, 2, 5, 1})\n        heap.push(value);\n    while (auto value = heap.pop())\n        std::cout << *value;\n    std::cout << \" empty=\" << !heap.pop() << \"\\n\";",heap),'1258 empty=1',
'Repeated minimum extraction produces a sorted sequence even though the internal heap array was only partially ordered.',
'Always choosing the left child can violate order when the right child is smaller.',
['Which child should a min-heap sift choose?','The smaller existing child','Always the left child','The most recently inserted child'],'The smaller child preserves the local minimum relation after a swap.',
'Test zero, one, and two elements, plus equal keys. Validate the heap after every pop against an independent multiset.');
add('heaps','priority-ties','Make scheduling ties deterministic',
'A priority queue orders tasks by a comparator. When output order matters, define a tie-breaker such as a monotonically assigned sequence number. Numeric priority alone does not specify the order of equal-priority tasks.\n\nThe standard priority_queue is a max-priority adaptor under its comparator convention; choose the comparison carefully for a minimum-first scheduler.',
cpp('functional iostream queue utility vector',"    using Task = std::pair<int, int>;\n    std::priority_queue<Task, std::vector<Task>, std::greater<Task>> tasks;\n    tasks.push({2, 0});\n    tasks.push({1, 2});\n    tasks.push({1, 1});\n    while (!tasks.empty()) {\n        auto [priority, sequence] = tasks.top();\n        tasks.pop();\n        std::cout << priority << \":\" << sequence << \" \";\n    }\n    std::cout << \"done\\n\";"),'1:1 1:2 2:0 done',
'Pair ordering uses priority first and sequence second, producing a deterministic order for the tied priority-one tasks.',
'A comparator with inverted meaning can silently make a min-first scheduler process the largest priority first.',
['What resolves equal priorities here?','The sequence number','The allocation address','The thread schedule'],'The second pair component is an explicit tie-breaker.',
'Replace pairs with a named Task type and an explicit comparator. Test equivalent priorities in several insertion orders.');
add('heaps','heap-reference','Use an independent sorted extraction oracle',
'A heap workload can be validated by collecting every extracted value and comparing it with an independently sorted copy of the inputs. This checks both ordering and multiplicity.\n\nCheck the structural invariant between operations as well, because a later operation might expose a corruption hidden by the current minimum.',
cpp('algorithm iostream optional utility vector',"    const std::vector<int> input{3, 1, 3, -2, 8};\n    MinHeap heap;\n    for (int value : input)\n        heap.push(value);\n    std::vector<int> actual;\n    bool valid = heap.valid();\n    while (auto value = heap.pop()) {\n        actual.push_back(*value);\n        valid = valid && heap.valid();\n    }\n    auto expected = input;\n    std::ranges::sort(expected);\n    valid = valid && actual == expected;\n    std::cout << \"verified=\" << valid << \"\\n\";\n    return valid ? 0 : 1;",heap),'verified=1',
'The reference retains duplicate threes and the negative value, while invariant checks cover every intermediate heap state.',
'Checking only that extracted values are sorted would accept a heap that dropped duplicates.',
['Why compare with the complete sorted input?','To check content and multiplicity as well as order','To reproduce sift-down','To require identical internal array positions'],'An independent sequence specifies every expected extraction.',
'Add interleaved push/pop sequences and compare the current minimum with a multiset after each step.');

const graph="using Graph = std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>>;\nstd::vector<int> distances(const Graph &graph, std::size_t start) {\n    std::vector<int> result(graph.size(), -1);\n    std::queue<std::size_t> work;\n    result.at(start) = 0;\n    work.push(start);\n    while (!work.empty()) {\n        auto node = work.front();\n        work.pop();\n        for (auto next : graph[node])\n            if (result.at(next) < 0) {\n                result[next] = result[node] + 1;\n                work.push(next);\n            }\n    }\n    return result;\n}";
add('graphs','adjacency-values','Own adjacency lists and validate vertex IDs',
'An adjacency list stores one neighbor sequence per vertex. The graph owns those sequences; traversal borrows them. Decide whether edges are directed and whether duplicate edges or self-loops are permitted.\n\nExternal vertex IDs must be validated before indexing. This fixture uses a small directed graph with vertices numbered from zero.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    const std::vector<std::vector<unsigned>> graph{{1, 2}, {2}, {}};\n    for (std::size_t vertex = 0; vertex < graph.size(); ++vertex)\n        std::cout << vertex << \":\" << graph[vertex].size() << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"vertices=\" << graph.size() << \"\\n\";"),'0:2 1:1 2:0 vertices=3',
'The out-degrees are two, one, and zero. An empty neighbor list is different from a missing vertex.',
'A neighbor ID outside the vertex range can become an invalid array access during traversal.',
['Does an empty adjacency list mean the vertex is absent?','No, it has no outgoing edges','Yes','Only in a directed graph'],'Vertex existence is determined by the outer representation.',
'Add a validation pass checking every edge endpoint before traversal. Test a disconnected vertex and a self-loop.');
add('graphs','breadth-first','Breadth-first traversal discovers shortest hop counts',
'In an unweighted graph, breadth-first search uses a FIFO queue to discover vertices by increasing hop distance. Marking a vertex when enqueued prevents duplicate work through multiple incoming paths.\n\nThe distance vector doubles as visited state: minus one means undiscovered, while zero is the source distance.',
cpp('iostream queue vector',"    Graph graph{{1, 2}, {3}, {3}, {}};\n    for (int distance : distances(graph, 0))\n        std::cout << distance;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";",graph),'0112',
'Vertices one and two are one hop from zero; vertex three is two hops away through either predecessor.',
'Marking visited only when dequeued can enqueue the same vertex many times.',
['When is a new vertex marked here?','When it is enqueued','Only after all traversal finishes','Whenever a duplicate edge is seen'],'Discovery establishes its shortest hop distance before enqueueing.',
'Add cycles and duplicate edges and verify the traversal still terminates. Compare distance results with hand-drawn shortest paths.');
add('graphs','depth-first','Depth-first traversal needs explicit visited state',
'Depth-first search follows one path before exploring siblings. In a graph, visited state prevents cycles from causing unbounded recursion. A tree-only traversal without visited state is not a general graph traversal.\n\nThis example uses a bounded recursive graph walk and prints a deterministic order defined by adjacency order.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    const std::vector<std::vector<unsigned>> graph{{1, 2}, {2}, {0}};\n    std::vector<bool> seen(graph.size());\n    visit(graph, 0, seen);\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";","void visit(const std::vector<std::vector<unsigned>> &graph, unsigned node,\n           std::vector<bool> &seen) {\n    if (seen.at(node))\n        return;\n    seen[node] = true;\n    std::cout << node;\n    for (auto next : graph[node])\n        visit(graph, next, seen);\n}"),'012',
'The cycle back to zero stops because zero was marked before following its outgoing edges.',
'Unbounded recursion depth can exhaust the stack even when visited state prevents cycles.',
['Why mark before visiting neighbors?','To stop a cycle from re-entering an active vertex','To sort all edges','To remove the source vertex'],'The mark prevents recursive re-entry through a back edge.',
'Rewrite the traversal with an explicit stack. State how pushing neighbors changes the observable visit order.');
add('graphs','topological-order','Acyclic dependencies admit a topological order',
'A topological order places every directed dependency before its dependent. Kahn’s algorithm starts with zero-indegree vertices, removes their outgoing edges logically, and enqueues newly available vertices.\n\nIf fewer than all vertices are emitted, a directed cycle prevents a complete order. The algorithm must report that outcome instead of treating a partial result as complete.',
cpp('iostream queue vector',"    const std::vector<std::vector<unsigned>> graph{{1, 2}, {3}, {3}, {}};\n    std::vector<unsigned> degree(graph.size());\n    for (const auto &edges : graph)\n        for (auto next : edges)\n            ++degree.at(next);\n    std::queue<unsigned> ready;\n    for (unsigned i = 0; i < degree.size(); ++i)\n        if (!degree[i])\n            ready.push(i);\n    unsigned count = 0;\n    while (!ready.empty()) {\n        auto node = ready.front();\n        ready.pop();\n        std::cout << node;\n        ++count;\n        for (auto next : graph[node])\n            if (--degree[next] == 0)\n                ready.push(next);\n    }\n    std::cout << \" complete=\" << (count == graph.size()) << \"\\n\";"),'0123 complete=1',
'The source becomes available first; its two dependents precede the final vertex that requires both.',
'A topological order is not guaranteed to be unique. Define a tie policy when reproducible output matters.',
['What signals a cycle in this algorithm?','Fewer emitted vertices than the graph contains','Any vertex has outgoing edges','More than one zero-indegree vertex exists'],'A remaining cycle prevents its vertices from reaching indegree zero.',
'Add an edge from the last vertex back to the first and verify incomplete processing is reported.');
add('graphs','weighted-path','Weighted shortest paths require a compatible algorithm',
'Breadth-first hop counts do not minimize arbitrary edge weights. For nonnegative weights, Dijkstra’s algorithm repeatedly settles the smallest tentative distance using a priority queue. Stale queue entries must be skipped.\n\nThe teaching fixture uses small integer weights with a bounded total. A general interface must check distance addition and reject negative weights.',
cpp('functional iostream limits queue utility vector',"    using Edge = std::pair<unsigned, int>;\n    const std::vector<std::vector<Edge>> graph{\n        {{1, 5}, {2, 1}}, {{3, 1}}, {{1, 1}, {3, 9}}, {}};\n    const int infinity = 1000000;\n    std::vector<int> distance(graph.size(), infinity);\n    distance[0] = 0;\n    using Item = std::pair<int, unsigned>;\n    std::priority_queue<Item, std::vector<Item>, std::greater<Item>> work;\n    work.push({0, 0});\n    while (!work.empty()) {\n        auto [cost, node] = work.top();\n        work.pop();\n        if (cost != distance[node])\n            continue;\n        for (auto [next, weight] : graph[node])\n            if (cost + weight < distance[next]) {\n                distance[next] = cost + weight;\n                work.push({distance[next], next});\n            }\n    }\n    std::cout << distance[1] << \" \" << distance[3] << \"\\n\";"),'2 3',
'The cheaper path reaches vertex one through vertex two, then reaches vertex three with total cost three.',
'Negative edge weights invalidate Dijkstra’s settling argument.',
['Which edge-weight assumption is required?','Nonnegative weights','All weights must equal one','Weights must be distinct'],'The smallest tentative distance can be safely advanced under the nonnegative-weight assumption.',
'Compare this result with all simple paths in the small fixture. Add input validation and checked distance addition before using external graph data.');
}
